    bio: "Buzurg ibn Shahriyar was a Persian sea captain and author from Ramhormuz — a port town at the head of the Persian Gulf — who spent much of his working life sailing the Indian Ocean routes between the Gulf, the coasts of India, and the East African shore. He was a practical mariner first, and a writer second, and the gap between those two roles gives his surviving work its distinctive, salt-encrusted texture. He is best known as the compiler of the Kitab Aja\'ib al-Hind, or 'The Book of the Marvels of India', a collection of seafarers' tales assembled from his own voyages and from stories told to him by other pilots and merchants at the ports and anchorages of the medieval Indian Ocean world.\n\nThe Kitab Aja\'ib al-Hind, completed around 953 AD, is one of the most extraordinary texts to survive from the medieval Islamic world. It contains 136 accounts of voyages, sea-monsters, strange islands, miraculous events, and encounters with foreign peoples, ranging from the plausible to the frankly fantastical. Modern scholars have spent considerable energy trying to disentangle the factual core from the legendary accretion, and the exercise is revealing: behind the sea-serpents and cannibal kings, there is a remarkably detailed and accurate picture of the Indian Ocean trading world of the ninth and tenth centuries, including the first Arab descriptions of the Swahili Coast and detailed accounts of the island of Madagascar.\n\nBuzurg\'s great gift was not systematic geography but vivid, first-hand narrative. His accounts of storms, near-shipwrecks, corrupt port officials, hospitable foreign kings, and the solidarity of sailors far from home carry a immediacy that no later compiler of geographical abstractions can match. He describes the grief of an elephant that mourned its dead keeper, the mechanical wonders of Indian courts, and the navigational knowledge of East African fishermen who could read the ocean floor by the colour of the water above it. Whether every detail is true matters less than the fact that the world he describes was real — a world of sustained, sophisticated, multilingual commerce that European historians largely ignored for centuries.\n\nBuzurg ibn Shahriyar has no tomb that anyone visits, no feast day, and no monument. His book survived in a single manuscript tradition and was not translated into a European language until the nineteenth century. But for historians of the Indian Ocean world, he is irreplaceable: a witness from the inside, a man who had actually been to the places he described, and who preserved the memory of an oceanic civilisation that was already ancient when Europe was just beginning to look beyond its own horizon."
